.TH "vsearch-sff" "5" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch file formats"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
sff \[em] a binary file format used to encode pyrosequencing results
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The standard flowgram format (sff), was designed by 454 Life Sciences,
the Whitehead Institute for Biomedical Research, and the Sanger
Institute.
It was used to store reads produced by the Roche 454 sequencing
platforms, and early versions of the Ion Torrent PGM sequencing
platforms.
.PP
Original NCBI documentation available at:
.PP
<https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/Traces/trace.cgi?view=doc_formats#sffhere>
.PP
If unavailable, it can be recovered from <https://web.archive.org>.
We provide this modified version for preservation purposes.
.PP
The proposed SFF file format (version 1) is a container file for storing
one or many 454 reads.
454 reads differ from standard sequencing reads in that the 454 data
does not provide individual base measurements from which basecalls can
be derived.
Instead, it provides measurements that estimate the length of the next
homopolymer stretch in the sequence (i.e., in \[lq]AAATGG\[rq],
\[lq]AAA\[rq] is a 3-mer stretch of A\[cq]s, \[lq]T\[rq] is a 1-mer
stretch of T\[cq]s and \[lq]GG\[rq] is a 2-mer stretch of G\[cq]s).
A basecalled sequence is then derived by converting each estimate into a
homopolymer stretch of that length and concatenating the homopolymers.
.PP
The file format consists of different sections: a \f[I]common
header\f[R] section occurring once in the file, then for each read
stored in the file, a \f[I]read header\f[R] section and a \f[I]read
data\f[R] section.
An optional \f[I]index\f[R] section can also be present after the
\f[I]common header\f[R] section, or anywhere among the reads, or at the
end of the file.
.PP
The data in each section consists of a combination of numeric and
character data, where the specific fields for each section are defined
below.
The sections adhere to the following rules:
.IP \[bu] 2
The standard Unix types \f[V]uint8_t\f[R], \f[V]uint16_t\f[R],
\f[V]uint32_t\f[R] and \f[V]uint64_t\f[R] are used to define 1, 2, 4 and
8-byte numeric values.
.IP \[bu] 2
All multi-byte numeric values are stored in \f[I]big endian\f[R] byte
order (also called network byte order; same as the Standard Chromatogram
Format, SCF).
\f[I]Endianness\f[R] refers to the order in which the bytes of a
multi-byte value are stored in a file or in memory: in \f[I]big
endian\f[R] order, the most significant byte is stored first, at the
lowest address.
This contrasts with \f[I]little endian\f[R] order, where the least
significant byte comes first \[em] the convention used by most modern
processors (x86, ARM).
.IP \[bu] 2
All character fields use single-byte ASCII characters.
.IP \[bu] 2
Each section definition ends with an \f[V]eight_byte_padding\f[R] field,
which consists of 0 to 7 bytes of padding, so that the length of each
section is divisible by 8 (and hence the next section is aligned on an
8-byte boundary).
.SS Common Header Section
.PP
The \f[I]common header\f[R] section consists of the following fields:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
 magic_number               uint32_t
 version                    char[4]
 index_offset               uint64_t
 index_length               uint32_t
 number_of_reads            uint32_t
 header_length              uint16_t
 key_length                 uint16_t
 number_of_flows_per_read   uint16_t
 flowgram_format_code       uint8_t
 flow_chars                 char[number_of_flows_per_read]
 key_sequence               char[key_length]
 eight_byte_padding         uint8_t[*]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
where the following properties are true for these fields:
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]magic_number\f[R] field value is 0x2E736666, the
\f[V]uint32_t\f[R] encoding of the string \[lq].sff\[rq]
.IP \[bu] 2
The version number corresponding to this proposal is 0001, or the byte
array \[lq]\[rs]0\[rs]0\[rs]0\[rs]1\[rq].
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]index_offset\f[R] and \f[V]index_length\f[R] fields are the
offset and length of an optional index of the reads in the SFF file.
If no index is included in the file, both fields must be 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]number_of_reads\f[R] field should be set to the number of reads
stored in the file.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]header_length\f[R] field should be the total number of bytes
required by this set of header fields, and should be equal to \[lq]31 +
\f[V]number_of_flows_per_read\f[R] + \f[V]key_length\f[R]\[rq] rounded
up to the next value divisible by 8.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]key_length\f[R] and \f[V]key_sequence\f[R] fields should be set
to the length and nucleotide bases of the key sequence used for these
reads.
Note: The \f[V]key_sequence\f[R] field is not null-terminated.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]number_of_flows_per_read\f[R] should be set to the number of
flows for each of the reads in the file.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]flowgram_format_code\f[R] should be set to the format used to
encode each of the flowgram values for each read.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Note: Currently, only one flowgram format has been adopted, so this
value should be set to 1.
.IP \[bu] 2
The flowgram format code 1 stores each value as a \f[V]uint16_t\f[R],
where the floating point flowgram value is encoded as \[lq](int)
round(value * 100.0)\[rq], and decoded as \[lq](storedvalue * 1.0 /
100.0)\[rq].
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]flow_chars\f[R] should be set to the array of nucleotide bases
(`A', `C', `G' or `T') that correspond to the nucleotides used for each
flow of each read.
The length of the array should equal \f[V]number_of_flows_per_read\f[R].
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Note: The \f[V]flow_chars\f[R] field is not null-terminated.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
If any \f[V]eight_byte_padding\f[R] bytes exist in the section, they
should have a byte value of 0.
.SS Read Header Section
.PP
The rest of the file contains the information about the reads, namely
\f[V]number_of_reads\f[R] entries consisting of \f[I]read header\f[R]
and \f[I]read data\f[R] sections.
The \f[I]read header\f[R] section consists of the following fields:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
 read_header_length         uint16_t
 name_length                uint16_t
 number_of_bases            uint32_t
 clip_qual_left             uint16_t
 clip_qual_right            uint16_t
 clip_adapter_left          uint16_t
 clip_adapter_right         uint16_t
 name                       char[name_length]
 eight_byte_padding         uint8_t[*]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
where these fields have the following properties:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]read_header_length\f[R] should be set to the length of the read
header for this read, and should be equal to \[lq]16 + name_length\[rq]
rounded up to the next value divisible by 8.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]name_length\f[R] and \f[V]name\f[R] fields should be set to the
length and string of the read\[cq]s accession or name.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Note: The name field is not null-terminated.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]number_of_bases\f[R] should be set to the number of bases
called for this read.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]clip_qual_left\f[R] and \f[V]clip_adapter_left\f[R] fields
should be set to the position of the first base after the clipping
point, for quality and/or an adapter sequence, at the beginning of the
read.
If only a combined clipping position is computed, it should be stored in
\f[V]clip_qual_left\f[R].
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
The position values use 1-based indexing, so the first base is at
position 1.
.IP \[bu] 2
If a clipping value is not computed, the field should be set to 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
Thus, the first base of the insert is:
.RS 2
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
max(
 1,
 max(clip_qual_left, clip_adapter_left)
   )
\f[R]
.fi
.RE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]clip_qual_right\f[R] and \f[V]clip_adapter_right\f[R] fields
should be set to the position of the last base before the clipping
point, for quality and/or an adapter sequence, at the end of the read.
If only a combined clipping position is computed, it should be stored in
\f[V]clip_qual_right\f[R].
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
The position values use 1-based indexing.
.IP \[bu] 2
If a clipping value is not computed, the field should be set to 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
Thus, the last base of the insert is:
.RS 2
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
min(
 (clip_qual_right    == 0 ? number_of_bases : clip_qual_right),
 (clip_adapter_right == 0 ? number_of_bases : clip_adapter_right)
   )
\f[R]
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SS Read Data Section
.PP
The read data section consists of the following fields:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
 flowgram_values            uint*_t[number_of_flows]
 flow_index_per_base        uint8_t[number_of_bases]
 bases                      char[number_of_bases]
 quality_scores             uint8_t[number_of_bases]
 eight_byte_padding         uint8_t[*]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
where the fields have the following properties:
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]flowgram_values\f[R] field contains the homopolymer stretch
estimates for each flow of the read.
The number of bytes used for each value depends on the common header
\f[V]flowgram_format_code\f[R] value (where the current value uses a
\f[V]uint16_t\f[R] for each value).
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]flow_index_per_base\f[R] field contains the flow positions for
each base in the called sequence (i.e., for each base, the position in
the flowgram whose estimate resulted in that base being called).
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
These values are \[lq]incremental\[rq] values, meaning that the stored
position is the offset from the previous flow index in the field.
.IP \[bu] 2
All position values (prior to their incremental encoding) use 1-based
indexing, so the first flow is flow 1.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]bases\f[R] field contains the basecalled nucleotide sequence.
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[V]quality_scores\f[R] field contains the quality scores for each
of the bases in the sequence, where the values use the standard -log10
probability scale.
.SS Index Section (optional)
.PP
If an index is included in the file, the \f[V]index_offset\f[R] and
\f[V]index_length\f[R] values in the \f[I]common header\f[R] should
point to the section of the file containing the index.
Note that the \f[I]index\f[R] section can be placed after the
\f[I]common header\f[R] section, or anywhere among the reads, or at the
end of the file.
To support different indexing methods, the index section should begin
with the following two fields:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
 index_magic_number         uint32_t
 index_version              char[4]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
followed by the actual index data (\f[I]n\f[R] bytes) and a
\f[V]eight_byte_padding\f[R] field, so that the total length of the
index section is divisible by 8.
The format of the rest of the index data is specific to the indexing
method used.
Currently, there are no officially supported indexing formats.
The \f[V]index_length\f[R] given in the common header should include the
bytes of these fields and the padding.
.SS Computing Lengths and Scanning the File
.PP
The length of each read\[cq]s section will be different, because of
different length accession numbers and different length nucleotide
sequences.
However, the various flow, name and bases lengths given in the common
and read headers can be used to scan the file, accessing each read\[cq]s
information or skipping read sections in the file.
The following pseudocode gives an example method to scanning the file
and accessing each read\[cq]s data:
.IP \[bu] 2
Open the file and/or reset the file pointer position to the first byte
of the file.
.IP \[bu] 2
Read the first 31 bytes of the file, confirm the \f[V]magic_number\f[R]
value and \f[V]version\f[R], then extract the \f[V]number_of_reads\f[R],
\f[V]number_of_flows_per_read\f[R], \f[V]flowgram_format_code\f[R],
\f[V]header_length\f[R], \f[V]key_length\f[R], \f[V]index_offset\f[R]
and \f[V]index_length\f[R] values.
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Convert the \f[V]flowgram_format_code\f[R] into a
\f[V]flowgram_bytes_per_flow\f[R] value (currently with format_code 1,
this value is 2 bytes).
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
If the \f[V]flow_chars\f[R] and \f[V]key_sequence\f[R] information is
required, read the next \[lq]\f[V]header_length\f[R] - 31\[rq] bytes,
then extract that information.
Otherwise, set the file pointer position to byte
\f[V]header_length\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
While the file contains more bytes, do the following:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
If the file pointer position equals \f[V]index_offset\f[R], either read
or skip \f[V]index_length\f[R] bytes in the file, processing the index
if read.
.IP \[bu] 2
Otherwise,
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Read 16 bytes and extract the \f[V]read_header_length\f[R],
\f[V]name_length\f[R] and \f[V]number_of_bases\f[R] values.
.IP \[bu] 2
Read the next \[lq]\f[V]read_header_length - 16\f[R]\[rq] bytes to read
the name.
.IP \[bu] 2
At this point, a test of the \f[V]name\f[R] field can be performed, to
determine whether to read or skip this entry.
.IP \[bu] 2
Compute the \f[V]read_data_length\f[R] as:
\f[V]number_of_flows *   flowgram_bytes_per_flow + 3 * number_of_bases\f[R],
rounded up to the next value divisible by 8.
.IP \[bu] 2
Either read or skip \f[V]read_data_length\f[R] bytes in the file,
processing the read data if the section is read.
.RE
.RE
.SH VSEARCH BEHAVIOUR
.PP
vsearch (\f[V]--sff_convert\f[R]) enforces a few restrictions beyond
this specification: it rejects files whose version is not 1, whose
flowgram format code is not 1, or whose key length is not 4 (the
specification allows any length, but 4 is universal in practice); an
index length between 1 and 7 bytes is also rejected as invalid.
An index located in the middle of the file triggers the warning
\[lq]Index at unusual position in file\[rq], and bytes following the
last read section trigger \[lq]Additional data at end of SFF file
ignored\[rq].
